- can only be done in directed graph

- cannot be done in cyclic graph

- Topological sorting for Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) is a linear ordering of vertices such that for every directed edge u-v, vertex u comes before v in the ordering

- topological sort is dfs but we store all the elements we visit in stack

- once we visit all the elements of u we put u in stack\

- so once we reach the last node in DFS loop wont run and last element will be added as it is, then we go back 

- in this way all the Vs come before U, thus maintaing the flow of elements being placed in order they occur


code : https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/topological-sort/1

class Solution {
    
    static void topo (int u, boolean[] vi, Stack<Integer> s, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj){
        vi[u] = true;
        
        for(int v : adj.get(u)) if(!vi[v]) topo(v, vi, s, adj);
        
        s.push(u);
    }
    
    static ArrayList<Integer> topologicalSort(ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {
        boolean[] v = new boolean[adj.size()];
        Stack<Integer> s = new Stack<>();
        
        for(int i = 0; i<adj.size(); i++) if(!v[i]) topo(i, v, s,adj);
        
        ArrayList<Integer> a = new ArrayList();
        
        while(!s.isEmpty()) a.add(s.pop());

        return a;
    }
}
